          "text": "{\"target_identification\":{\"speaker\":\"The rose\",\"addressee\":\"The little prince\",\"anchor_quote\":\"je t'aime\"},\"target_translation\":\"I love you.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"The rose directly avows as true her loving emotional state toward the little prince. Her admission that he had not known it because of her fault reinforces the utterance's truth-presenting, confessional force.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"je t'aime\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Tu n'en a rien su, par ma faute.\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.96,\"reason\":\"The focal expression avows love but does not itself undertake a future action, obligation, or relational commitment. The rose undertakes nothing through these words; the surrounding wishes for his happiness and relinquishment of the protective globe should not be transferred onto the focal avowal.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"je t'aime\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Tâche d'être heureux...Laisse ce globe tranquille. Je n'en veux plus.\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.9,\"reason\":\"Although the rose is affected and later conceals tears, the focal words are represented as a calm, articulate disclosure rather than a reflex-like or uncontrolled affective discharge.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Il ne comprenait pas cette douceur calme.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Car elle ne voulait pas qu'il la vît pleurer.\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.94,\"reason\":\"The focal expression's core force is adequately captured as an avowal of a loving state. The supplied scene does not support an additional core function outside T/P/E.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"je t'aime\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"At the little prince's departure, after apologizing for her earlier conduct, the rose calmly tells him that she loves him and accepts blame for his not having known it. The admission occurs within a farewell in which she urges him to be happy and conceals her tears.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"The focal expression contains no undertaking by the rose; it is a direct avowal. The surrounding turn releases the little prince from tending the rose and urges him to be happy, but those neighbouring acts are not commitments encoded by “je t'aime.” The supplied scene gives no explicit verbal acknowledgment of the avowal by the little prince before the dialogue moves on.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"Recognised as Antoine de Saint-Exupéry's Le Petit Prince (The Little Prince), specifically the rose's farewell to the little prince.\",\"limitations\":\"The assessment is confined to the marked occurrence and supplied passages. They strongly support an avowal and actual affection, but do not establish any future relational terms or explicit uptake beyond the represented scene.\"}"
